I will replace Strategies 1 and 2 with a unified "Interesting Pairs" approach that:
1.  Generates a robust set of "good" exponents including small numbers, units, AND divisors.
2.  Constructs pairs based on doubling ($u, 2u$), tripling ($u, 3u$), and near-doubling ($u, 2u \pm 1$).
3.  Applies these pairs to both the x/y-swap strategy and the constant-monomial strategy.

This change aims to capture the specific algebraic structure of the best-known codes while maintaining diversity.

